{"title":"Correspondence","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>A <b>correspondence</b> is a relation between two <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Set\" title=\"Set\">sets</a> such that each member in one set corresponds to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> members in the other set, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> commonly equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Lists_-_the_beginning\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Lists - the beginning</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#One-to-One_Correspondence\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">One-to-One Correspondence</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Examples\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Examples</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Intermediate\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Intermediate</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Lists_-_the_beginning\">Lists - the beginning</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Correspondence&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Lists - the beginning\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider the task of counting the number of integers between 14 and 103 inclusive.  We could simply list those <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Integers\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Integers\">integers</a> and count them.  However, we can renumber those integers so that they correspond to the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Counting_numbers\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Counting numbers\">counting numbers</a> (positive integers), starting with 1.  In this correspondence, 14 corresponds to 1 (for the 1st integer in the list), 15 with 2, 16 with 3, etc.  The relationship between the members of each pair is that the second is 13 less than the first.  So, we know that 103 corresponds to the 103 - 13 = 90th integer in the list.  Thus, the list is 90 integers long.\n</p><p>Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/5/745ddd00ed8649772474b92b9d6e0639386fea2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$13 = 14 - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"91\" height=\"13\" />, or 1 less than the first integer in the list.  If we start our list with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and end with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> (i.e. m and n inclusive), the number of integers in the list is \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/f/99f62af908591db706c76b893214c7e309582c7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m - (n -1) = m - n + 1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"202\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"One-to-One_Correspondence\">One-to-One Correspondence</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Correspondence&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: One-to-One Correspondence\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<dl><dd><i>Main article: <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Bijection\" title=\"Bijection\">Bijection</a></i></dd></dl>\n<p>A <b>one-to-one correspondence</b>, or <i>bijection</i>, is a function which is both <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Injection\" title=\"Injection\">injective</a> (or <i>one-to-one</i>) and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Surjection\" title=\"Surjection\">surjective</a> (or <i>onto</i>).  A function has a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Function#The_Inverse_of_a_Function\" title=\"Function\">two-sided inverse</a> exactly when it is a bijection between its <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Domain\" title=\"Domain\">domain</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Range\" title=\"Range\">range</a>.\n</p><p>One-to-one correspondences are useful in a variety of contexts.  In particular, bijections are frequently used in <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Combinatorics\" title=\"Combinatorics\">combinatorics</a> in order to count the elements of a set whose size is unknown.  Bijections are also very important in <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Set_theory\" title=\"Set theory\">set theory</a> when dealing with arguments concerning <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinite\" title=\"Infinite\">infinite</a> sets.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Examples\">Examples</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Correspondence&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Examples\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Correspondence&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Intermediate\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2006 AIME II Problems/Problem 4\">2006 AIME II Problems/Problem 4</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2001 AIME I Problems/Problem 6\">2001 AIME I Problems/Problem 6</a></li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Correspondence&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Combinatorics\" title=\"Combinatorics\">Combinatorics</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017224314\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.020 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.040 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 73/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 68/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 36/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 5/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 1229/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   14.380      1 Template:Main\n100.00%   14.380      1 -total\n 41.25%    5.932      1 Template:Hatnote\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:1803:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017224314 and rev